### Typography — LaTeX, selectively

Install KaTeX and load its stylesheet. It ships Computer Modern clones as web fonts, so one dependency gives both the LaTeX look and real rendered maths.

```css
--serif: 'KaTeX_Main', 'Latin Modern Roman', Georgia, serif;
--mono:  ui-monospace, 'SF Mono', Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
```

| Where | Face | Size |
|---|---|---|
| Item titles, summaries, notes, debt entries | `--serif` | 15–17px |
| Task and action text | `--serif` | 15px |
| Ids, status words, dates, counts, ageing, section labels | `--mono` | 10–12px |
| Buttons and filters | `--mono` | 11px |

**Do not put Computer Modern below 15px.** It was cut for print at high resolution and goes thin and muddy on screen — it is the single most common way this look goes wrong. Every small label stays monospace, which also preserves the hairline-and-rule character of the existing systems map.

Set `font-feature-settings: "liga" 1, "onum" 1` on the serif for old-style figures in running prose. Keep lining figures in the monospace so counts and dates align in columns.

**Inline maths.** Build a `<Tex>{'\\delta_c'}</Tex>` wrapper over `katex.renderToString` with `throwOnError: false`. It is needed immediately — δ_c ≈ 0.1952 ≈ 1/√27, the β-plane, τ ≥ 7 days, AC(1) = 0.994, z = +4.06, 2⁴ = 16. Rendering those properly rather than as Unicode approximations is most of why the LaTeX direction is right here, and it is the detail that reads as considered rather than decorative.

Structure: hairline rules, square corners, **no border radius anywhere**, no shadows, no gradients. Progress bars are flat filled rectangles, not rounded pills.

Status colours: `published` moss · `in_review` cold · `active` cold · `blocked` warm · `not_started` graphite · `paused` hair.

Responsive at 900px (grid and panel stack) and 620px (single-column cards).

