## 10. Links out, and the thesis-debt register

Two additions that turn this from a tracker into something that earns its keep at submission.

### 10.1 Links point out; they do not hold

Each item carries a `links` array. The site **indexes** material and does not store it. Code stays in git, figures and data get a Zenodo DOI at publication, manuscripts live in their own repos, PDFs sit with the publisher.

```ts
links: [
  { kind:'repo',       label:'Analysis code', url:'https://github.com/…/sst-circulation' },
  { kind:'figures',    label:'Figures 1–9',   url:'…', note:'Regenerate with make figs' },
  { kind:'data',       label:'Zenodo',        url:'…', note:'DOI minted at acceptance' },
  { kind:'manuscript', label:'Overleaf',      url:'…' },
]
```

Render as a compact monospace list at the foot of the detail panel. That is the whole feature — a field and a list, not an architecture.

**The thesis itself is a LaTeX repo, not a page on this site.** Writing it here would mean rebuilding `\ref`, BibTeX, equation numbering, the HUJI template and track-changes with Assaf, and arriving somewhere worse than Overleaf after months of work. The site links to that repo like it links to everything else.

### 10.2 The debt register

This is the part that saves real time, and it does not exist in any other document.

Thesis assembly is not expensive because files are hard to find. It is expensive because of the inconsistencies that only appear once chapters sit side by side — a domain fixed one way in medwr-4-7 and another in icon-attribution, a convention corrected in sst-circulation that beta-plane-paldor never picked up. Each one costs re-run figures, and by 2028 nobody remembers why the choice was made.

So record the decision at the moment it is taken, while the reasoning is still live. Seed these now:

- **MedWR domain 20–60°N** (medwr-4-7) — widening to 20–75°N broke classifiability. Any chapter using MedWR must match, or justify diverging. Affects icon-attribution, pds-precursor.
- **k = 8 for MedWR** (medwr-4-7) — affects icon-attribution, pds-precursor.
- **CCM direction convention** (sst-circulation) — the plotting convention had both directions swapped in every figure before correction. Any later CCM figure must state the convention explicitly. Affects ccm-failure-modes.
- **Preprocessing symmetry rule** (sst-circulation) — all CCM inputs preprocessed identically, anomalies throughout, never raw against anomaly. Affects ccm-failure-modes.
- **DSM continuity** (medwr-4-7) — computed over an unbroken series, subset afterwards. Affects icon-attribution.
- **SST baseline: HadISST for regimes, ERA5 for atmosphere** (sst-circulation) — affects medwr-4-7, icon-attribution, cmip-projections.
- **Regime numbering R1–R4** (sst-circulation) — the labels must not drift between chapters. Affects medwr-4-7, icon-attribution, cmip-projections.
- **Stein–Alpert factor separation** (icon-attribution) — affects cmip-projections.
- **Narrative frame: sub-seasonal predictability sources, not ocean forcing** — affects every chapter and the general introduction.

Surface unresolved debt in two places: a count on the card, and a **Debt** section in the detail panel below the tasks. Add a sixth filter, **Debt**, showing only items with unresolved entries.

Ten minutes per paper to seed. It is the difference between a hard month of synthesis and a brutal one.

### 10.3 What this does not remove

The site makes assembly cheap. It does not make the thesis short. You still owe a general introduction, a linking narrative explaining why sst-circulation → medwr-4-7 → beta-plane-paldor → icon-attribution → cmip-projections is one argument rather than five papers in a folder, a unified literature review, and a general discussion that says something the individual papers do not. beta-plane-paldor in particular needs a paragraph justifying why equatorial β-plane dynamics sits beside Atlantic SST forcing — Assaf has already flagged that the committee will ask.

That synthesis is the actual thesis work and no archive removes a day of it. Worth knowing now rather than in 2028.

## 11. The portfolio layer

The private site sits behind Cloudflare Access and contains unpublished research, so it cannot be shown to anyone. That is a problem for a portfolio piece, and it has a clean solution.

**Build a public demo from the same codebase.** `src/data/demo.ts` holds a synthetic register — invented projects, plausible shape, no real content. Select the dataset with an env var:

```ts
export const register = import.meta.env.VITE_DATASET === 'demo'
  ? demoRegister : realRegister;
```

Two Cloudflare Pages projects from one repo: the real one behind Access, the demo public with `VITE_DATASET=demo`. The demo URL goes in Bernadette's portfolio and any application.

**What makes this a strong piece is not feature count.** It is evidence of judgement, and the judgement is already in this brief — choosing a static file over a database, refusing to build auth, choosing to link out rather than store. Write those down. A short case study covering the problem, the constraints, the three or four decisions and what was deliberately left out is worth more than any number of additional features. Most portfolios show what someone built; very few show why they stopped.

Concretely:

- **README** — problem, decisions, what was rejected and why, screenshots, run instructions.
- **CASE_STUDY.md** — 600–900 words, the version to link from a CV.
- **Accessibility, done properly** — keyboard navigation throughout, visible focus, semantic buttons, `aria-expanded` on the cards, `prefers-reduced-motion` respected, contrast checked. Say so in the README. It is the most reliable signal of care in a frontend portfolio and it is cheap here.
- **A few real tests** — progress derives from `tasks`; the ageing counter is correct across a month boundary; filters do not clear the selection; `#beta-plane-paldor` selects that item on load. Four tests that check behaviour beat forty that check rendering.
- **Clean commit history** — small commits with real messages. People read these.

**Scope discipline still applies.** With a baby due in January, this is a one-weekend build plus a weekend of polish. Everything in Sections 10 and 11 fits that. Anything beyond it does not, and a tracker that becomes a platform becomes a tracker nobody updates — which is worse than none, because it looks authoritative while being wrong.

## 12. How Victor updates it

This was underspecified in revision 1 and it matters, because a tracker that is awkward to update stops being updated, and a stale tracker is worse than none.

**Victor does not run a dev environment.** He is not cloning the repo, running `npm install`, or working locally. The update path has to work from a browser.

**The path: GitHub's web editor.** Open `src/data/register.ts` on github.com, click the pencil, edit, commit. Pages rebuilds in under a minute. No local setup, no terminal, no git knowledge beyond clicking commit. It also gives full history and a one-click revert if something breaks.

To make that real rather than theoretical, add an **Edit** link to each item's detail panel:

```
https://github.com/{owner}/{repo}/edit/main/src/data/register.ts
```

Put the owner and repo in one constant. It is one line of code and it is the difference between updating taking ten seconds and requiring Victor to remember where the file lives.

**Guard against the obvious failure.** A syntax error in `register.ts` breaks the build and the site goes blank. Two cheap protections:

- Wrap the data import in a try/catch and render a plain error message naming the likely cause — a missing comma, or an unescaped apostrophe — rather than a white screen.
- Add a GitHub Action running `tsc --noEmit` on push. It catches the error before Pages deploys and emails him.

**What he will actually change, in order of frequency:** flipping `done` on a task; adding a task; updating `updated`; adding a link; adding or resolving a debt entry; changing `court` and `status`. Keep those fields near the top of each object so they are the first thing visible when the file opens.

**Not worth building:** an in-app editor. It means auth, a write path, and a database, and it replaces a workflow that already gives version history and rollback for free. If updating still proves too awkward after a month of real use, that is the point to reconsider — with evidence rather than in advance.

